# Round fxb (card for the dump sitting): ik's MTP draft dump of the glmq2k fixture variant (/models/fixture-variants/glmq2k/glm5next:
# GLM's fixture whose NextN layer alone is retyped to UD-Q2_K_XL's q2_K gate and up and q3_K down, scope nextn:
# tools/fixture-variants.tsv), the one set gate-gpu-glm5next-mtp reads on it: `BLOOMERY_TIER=fixture
# BLOOMERY_FIXTURE_VARIANT=glmq2k just dump-ref-mtp-fixture glm5next` under this card, one lease. Nothing is timed. The dumper is
# main's shared bin/ (the ik build the real and fixture sets were dumped with); a variant changes the file, not the dumper.
# Minutes [derived from the glm5next MTP fixture dump's term in glmmix-dump.card's family, a ~7.8 GB file read once and run on
# ik's CPU at -t 32 for a short prompt]: one lease take 3-15 s, the dump 0.1-0.3 min, the file's cold read 4-8 s, so 0.3-0.7 min,
# rounded up to 1..3. The requant (`just fixture-requant glm5next glmq2k`) takes no lease.
kind: exclusive
reason: dump_mtp runs ik on 32 CPU threads (CUDA hidden, no card); beside a timed run or a gate batch it would take their cores
  and memory bandwidth, and the profile takes the lease for every dump; why now: UD-Q2_K_XL's q2_K and q3_K routed layer is held
  by no fixture, so gate-gpu-glm5next-mtp has no oracle set on a file with that stack until ik has dumped the variant, and the
  real Q2_K_XL file may then leave /models
minutes: 1..3
